  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A weak front and surface low lift slowly back to the \nnorth today, bringing greater coverage of showers and storms to the \nregion. Locally heavy rainfall is the primary hazard. \n\nMorning analysis shows a weak stationary front draped across the \nregion from near South Hill northeastward into the MD Eastern Shore. \nAloft, a closed low is noted to the SW of the area in central GA. \nDeep southerly flow on the eastern flank of the upper low continues \nto funnel moisture northward into the Carolinas and along the \nstalled frontal boundary. PW values are 2.2-2.4\" across eastern NC \nbut taper to around 1.75\" along the boundary. Some showers with \noccasional thunder have been persistent along and west of the I-85 \ncorridor this morning. These showers should linger for a few more \nhours before dissipating. \n\nA subtle shortwave trough embedded in the upper level southerly\nflow will spawn a weak surface low in central NC early this \nmorning. The low and attendant front are forecast to lift slowly\nnorthward through the day with increasing chances for showers \nand storms expected, mainly along and south of the boundary. \nIncreasing deep layer moisture spreads back to the north as well\nwith the 00z HREF showing fairly impressive 30-50% probs for \n3\"/3 hours, mainly centered along and east of I-95 and south of \nI-64 this afternoon. Contemplated issuing a small Flood Watch \nfor these areas but recent rainfall has largely spared this \nregion with 3 hour flash flood guidance generally in the 3-4\" \nrange. However, given the deep moisture, weak flow aloft, and \ntall/skinny instability profiles noted on forecast soundings, \nthe potential for localized areas of flash flooding is apparent,\nespecially in urban/poor drainage areas. WPC has maintained \nMarginal ERO for the entire area today. The weak winds aloft \ncombined with widespread clouds will limit shear/instability and\nthe chance for severe weather across the region today and \ntonight. Temperatures will mainly be in the low to mid 80s with \ncloud cover and showers/storms lingering into tonight. Warm and \nvery muggy overnight with lows in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Convective coverage is expected to decrease \nWednesday and especially late week into the weekend with warmer \ntemperatures returning to the area.\n\n00z guidance has trended downward with respect to convective \ncoverage on Wednesday. Isolated showers/storms are possible but not \nexpecting widespread severe or flooding issues. With fewer clouds \nand showers around, temperatures will rebound into the mid and upper \n80s on Wednesday. Precip coverage is expected to further decrease \nlate week into the weekend as the upper trough to the west lifts \nnorthward and away from the area. Slight chance PoPs each day are \nmostly confined to western portions of the region in the afternoon \nand early evening hours. With lower shower and storm coverage, \ntemperatures return into the upper 80s and low 90s Thursday through \nSunday. Afternoon heat index values climb back into the upper 90s \nand low 100s late week into the weekend. While apparent temps may \nbriefly creep into Heat Advisory range (105+) in the afternoon, am \nnot expecting the need for heat headlines at this time.",
